-- title:
--   Krull dimension at least two for the completed node ring
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a positive integer $N$, a perfect field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$, together with modular polynomial data $data$ for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence $hKr$, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ for the Hecke series $\overline{\alpha}$, $\overline{\beta}$ at level $N$ and $q$, a place specialisation $P$ for these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ and $w$ a place of the modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ N$ over $k$. Consider the ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$, the subring of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (N q)$ consisting of those $f$ lying in $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\ w$ whose Laurent expansion lies in the field $\mathrm{NodeLocalized.fieldOver}\ (Nq)\ K$, and assume it is local and Noetherian. Let $c$ be a system of node coordinates for $R$ over $K$ at $w$, i.e. elements $x, y$ of that ring with $\mathrm{nodeResidue}_1(x) = 0$, $\mathrm{ord}_{\varphi w}(\mathrm{nodeResidue}_2(x)) = 1$ for the arithmetic-Frobenius translate of $w$, $\mathrm{nodeResidue}_2(y) = 0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_w(\mathrm{nodeResidue}_1(y)) = 1$. Let $\varpi$ lie in $A \cap K$, mapped into the node ring by the constants homomorphism $R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\ K\ w$. If the ideal generated by $\varpi$ and $x$ is prime and does not contain $y$, then the Krull dimension of the completion of $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$ along its maximal ideal is at least $2$.
--
--   This records that the local ring of $X_0(Nq)$ at a supersingular node, in the node-descent model over $K$, has completed Krull dimension at least two, the lower bound needed before identifying that completion with a crossing model $uv = \varpi^e$. It is used in the analysis of the two branches through the node, in particular by the statement producing an exponent and an isomorphism of the completion with the $uv$-crossing model, and in the subsequent slope and angular-factor computations.
